Task 1: Configure HTTPS for Prometheus
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Tools and Dependencies
First, we need to install Prometheus and the necessary tools for generating SSL certificates.

# Update the system package list
sudo apt update

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y wget curl openssl nginx

# Create a dedicated user for Prometheus
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus

# Create necessary directories
sudo mkdir -p /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus /etc/ssl/prometheus

# Set proper ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus
Subtask 1.2: Download and Install Prometheus
# Download the latest Prometheus release
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.47.0/prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
tar xvf prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Copy binaries to system directories
sudo cp prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64/prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64/promtool /usr/local/bin/

# Copy console files
sudo cp -r prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64/consoles /etc/prometheus/
sudo cp -r prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64/console_libraries /etc/prometheus/

# Set proper ownership and permissions
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/prometheus /usr/local/bin/promtool
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/consoles /etc/prometheus/console_libraries
Subtask 1.3: Generate SSL Certificates
We will create self-signed certificates for this lab. In production environments, you should use certificates from a trusted Certificate Authority.

# Generate private key
sudo openssl genrsa -out /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key 2048

# Generate certificate signing request
sudo openssl req -new -key /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key -out /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.csr -subj "/C=US/ST=State/L=City/O=Organization/CN=localhost"

# Generate self-signed certificate
sudo openssl x509 -req -days 365 -in /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.csr -signkey /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key -out /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt

# Set proper permissions
sudo chmod 600 /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key
sudo chmod 644 /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/ssl/prometheus/
Subtask 1.4: Create Prometheus Configuration with TLS
Create a secure Prometheus configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Add the following configuration:

global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s

rule_files:
  # - "first_rules.yml"
  # - "second_rules.yml"

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
    scheme: https
    tls_config:
      cert_file: /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt
      key_file: /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key
      insecure_skip_verify: true

  - job_name: 'node'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']

# TLS and authentication configuration
tls_server_config:
  cert_file: /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt
  key_file: /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key
Set proper ownership:

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Subtask 1.5: Create Prometheus Service File
Create a systemd service file for Prometheus:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
    --config.file /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
    --storage.tsdb.path /var/lib/prometheus/ \
    --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \
    --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries \
    --web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:9090 \
    --web.enable-lifecycle

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Subtask 1.6: Start and Test HTTPS Configuration
# Reload systemd and start Prometheus
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable prometheus
sudo systemctl start prometheus

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status prometheus

# Verify Prometheus is listening on port 9090
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 9090

# Test HTTPS connection (accept self-signed certificate)
curl -k https://localhost:9090/metrics
Task 2: Set up Authentication and RBAC for Secure Access
Subtask 2.1: Install and Configure Nginx as Reverse Proxy
We will use Nginx as a reverse proxy to add authentication capabilities to Prometheus.

# Install apache2-utils for htpasswd
sudo apt install -y apache2-utils

# Create authentication file
sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd admin

# You will be prompted to enter a password. Use a strong password like: SecurePass123!

# Add additional users if needed
sudo htpasswd /etc/nginx/.htpasswd viewer
# Use password: ViewerPass456!

# Verify the authentication file
sudo cat /etc/nginx/.htpasswd
Subtask 2.2: Configure Nginx Virtual Host
Remove the default Nginx configuration and create a new one:

# Remove default configuration
sudo rm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default

# Create new configuration for Prometheus
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/prometheus
Add the following configuration:

upstream prometheus {
    server 127.0.0.1:9090;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name localhost;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key;

    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA512:DHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA512:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:DHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384;
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;

    location / {
        auth_basic "Prometheus Authentication";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;

        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        
        # Handle WebSocket connections
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    }

    # Admin-only endpoints
    location ~ ^/(api/v1/admin|api/v1/write) {
        auth_basic "Admin Access Required";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
        
        # Only allow admin user
        if ($remote_user != "admin") {
            return 403;
        }
        
        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}

# Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;
}
Subtask 2.3: Enable and Configure Nginx
# Enable the site
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/prometheus /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

# Test Nginx configuration
sudo nginx -t

# Start and enable Nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx

# Check Nginx status
sudo systemctl status nginx
Subtask 2.4: Configure Advanced RBAC with Multiple User Roles
Create a more sophisticated authentication setup with different access levels:

# Create separate authentication files for different roles
sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd-admin admin
# Password: AdminPass123!

sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd-viewer viewer
# Password: ViewerPass456!

sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd-readonly readonly
# Password: ReadOnlyPass789!
Update the Nginx configuration for role-based access:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/prometheus
Replace the content with this enhanced configuration:

upstream prometheus {
    server 127.0.0.1:9090;
}

map $remote_user $user_role {
    default "none";
    "admin" "admin";
    "viewer" "viewer";
    "readonly" "readonly";
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name localhost;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/prometheus/prometheus.key;

    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA512:DHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA512:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:DHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384;
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;

    # Admin access - full permissions
    location ~ ^/(api/v1/admin|api/v1/write|api/v1/label/__name__/values) {
        auth_basic "Admin Access Required";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd-admin;
        
        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Role $user_role;
    }

    # Viewer access - read and query permissions
    location ~ ^/(api/v1/query|api/v1/query_range|api/v1/series|graph|targets) {
        auth_basic "Viewer Access Required";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
        
        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Role $user_role;
    }

    # Read-only access - metrics and status only
    location ~ ^/(metrics|api/v1/status) {
        auth_basic "Authentication Required";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
        
        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Role $user_role;
    }

    # Default location - requires authentication
    location / {
        auth_basic "Prometheus Authentication";
        auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
        
        proxy_pass https://prometheus;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Role $user_role;
    }
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;
}
